  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 921 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nActive weather will continue for a few more hours this evening\nthen begin taper off and dissipate in the early morning hours.\nThe band of showers and weak embedded thunderstorms is associated\nwith a strong Pacific front that is part of a larger weather\nsystem moving from west to east across the region. After the\npassage of the frontal system, conditions will turn dry and breezy\nto windy for the lowlands throughout the day on Monday. \n\nStronger winds aloft will generate very strong winds along the \neast slopes of the Sacramento mountains in the early morning hours\nthrough mid day. Wind gusts up to 70 mph may occur along the east\nslopes of the Sacramento Mountains. A high wind warning is in \neffect for that area. Temperatures will also be cooler behind the \nfront with lowland temperatures in the upper 60s to 70 degrees. \nTuesday will also be cooler but a mild warming trend will occur by\nmid week. \n\nAn extended period of quieter dry weather will occur the rest of\nthe week and into next weekend. Predominately westerly flow \npattern aloft will be in place. Low pressure troughs and short \nwave ridges will transit through the region with little impact on \nsouthern New Mexico and far west Texas. The westerly flow breaks \ndown next weekend and is replaced by strong ridge that will \nmaintain the dry pattern for the region. Tuesday through Sunday \nwill be mostly sunny, with light winds and seasonal temperatures.",
